#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# conway_polynomial

#% text
# `conway_polynomial(p, n, x)` gives the Conway polynomial of degree `n` over the integers modulo the prime `p`, in the variable `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The Conway polynomial is the least monic primitive polynomial, in a fixed order of the coefficients, whose roots map to roots of the Conway polynomials of the subfields under `a -> a^((p^n - 1)/(p^d - 1))`. It defines `finite_field(p, n)`.

#% item
# The polynomials are computed when first needed (a search for prime `n`, an enumeration of a coset of compatible elements for composite `n`) or read from a table; when neither is possible within the limits the result is a failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
conway_polynomial(2, 8, x)
#% output
#= 1 + x^2 + x^3 + x^4 + x^8

#% cell
conway_polynomial(101, 3, t)
#% output
#= 99 + 3*t + t^3

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# It is primitive:

#% cell
is_primitive_polynomial(conway_polynomial(3, 6, x), 3)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Degree 1: `x` minus the least primitive root:

#% cell
[conway_polynomial(31, 1, x), primitive_root(31)]
#% output
#= [28 + x, 3]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `finite_field`, `is_primitive_polynomial`, `is_irreducible_polynomial`
